+TUI and Desktop store prepared notifications with their subscription cursor in one
+board transaction. Pending notifications can recover after a process restart or
+closed session. Inspect them without changing the board or retrying a model turn:
+`hermes kanban notify-receipts --session <stored-session-id> --json` (use `hermes -p
+<profile>` for its owning profile, and `kanban --board <slug>` for another board).
+`pending` is retryable when the session becomes idle; `claimed`, `admitted` and
+`unknown` never replay automatically because a turn may already have started.
+`settled`, `failed` and `cancelled` are immutable terminal receipts, not task
+approval. An absent receipt proves neither delivery nor failure. This does not open a
+closed session, provide a scheduled attention producer, or guarantee exactly-once
+model execution. User Stop still holds pending work until the next user prompt.
+Unsubscribing (including archive/retention cleanup) cancels pending receipts in
+the same transaction; re-adding the route does not revive them. Already claimed
+work keeps its receipt because it may have started.
+
+After compression rotates a session ID, its selected continuation can consume the
+original subscription and pending receipts in the same owning profile. Branches,
+delegated/reset children, other profiles and stale parent runtimes do not inherit
+that route. Ancestor subscriptions require a matching notifier-profile stamp;
+unowned legacy routes retain exact-key delivery only. Receipt IDs, original stored
+session IDs and claim tokens are not retargeted: inspect `notify-receipts` with the
+original destination ID. Stop, unsubscribe and ambiguous-claim rules still apply.
+
+#### Daily attention for an existing TUI plan
+
+`hermes -p <profile> kanban attention-request <task-id> --day YYYY-MM-DD
+--session <stored-session-id> --guard <trusted-policy.json> --message "Inspect the existing plan" --json`
+commits a genuine `attention_requested` event, one daily receipt and its pending TUI
+delivery in the same board transaction. It preserves the task's status, assignee,
+claims, body and gates. The daily key is board + project workspace + Bangkok day +
+task ID. A repeated identical request returns the original receipt; changing its
+destination, message or policy is a conflict, including after cancellation or failure.
+Only today's Bangkok date at or after 07:00 is accepted.
+
+The version 1 policy is deliberately narrow. It must come from a **trusted reviewed
+automation template**, not be generated by hashing whatever happens to be on a card:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "workspace": "/absolute/project/path",
+  "created_by": "your-reviewed-automation",
+  "idempotency_key": "your-reviewed-automation:YYYY-MM-DD",
+  "body_sha256": "<64 lowercase hexadecimal characters from the trusted template>",
+  "initial_block_reason": "<exact routine planning request, not an owner decision>",
+  "initial_comment_author": "<the automation's CLI profile>"
+}
+```
+
+Only unassigned, never-executed `ready`, `todo`, or initially `blocked` plans matching
+that template qualify. A single routine `needs_input` classification and its exact
+automatic comment may qualify; other comments, dependencies, reviews, execution
+history, unknown events or changed content fail closed. There is no general
+`OwnerGATE` field: this contract does **not** infer consent from natural language or
+authorize external project holds. Callers must retain those separate project gates.
+An existing wake-capable TUI subscription must belong to the invoking profile.
+
+The consumer rechecks eligibility, the captured task fingerprint, date and route in
+its claim transaction. Changes before that claim cancel the pending delivery. A claim
+is the admission boundary; subsequent edits do not prove a started turn was stopped.
+Inspect `notify-receipts` for its disposition. Known refusals may retry while eligible;
+claimed/unknown outcomes never replay automatically. This command does not open a
+closed captain, install a timer, publish work, or provide a scheduled execution ACK.
+
+#### Explicit host for one closed captain request
+
+`hermes -p <profile> kanban attention-run <delivery-id> --session <original-stored-session-id> --json`
+explicitly runs **one existing daily attention receipt** in its closed local chat. Merely
+queuing attention still never opens a session. The host follows only the selected
+compression continuation and retains the original receipt identity. It restores the
+stored model/provider, reasoning, service tier, workspace and canonical model history.
+The primary must be explicitly configured; both resolution-time and model fallback
+ladders are disabled for this command.
+
+The host uses the existing active-session cap and exclusive writer lease. A live
+owner anywhere on that compression continuation, Kanban worker provenance, a missing
+workspace, or a changed task/route/date refuses admission. Eligibility is rechecked
+inside the receipt claim transaction. Only that receipt is submitted; no ordinary
+resume, notification polling, old crash continuation, goal judge, loop tick, completion
+queue drain, automatic title generation or unattended prompt suggestion is started. Normal tools within the one
+requested agent turn retain their ordinary approval and project gates. This command
+does not approve a task, publication, or an external owner hold.
+
+The native agent persists the answer into the same session. The host retries the
+idempotent transcript flush before the terminal receipt; a reported flush failure is
+`unknown`. `settled` means a completed turn, not task approval or provider-side memory
+write success. Interrupted/partial/failed results are not settled. An exception or
+process crash after receipt claim may leave `claimed` or `unknown`; neither is
+replayed automatically, including on a repeated command. Inspect `notify-receipts`
+with the **original** destination ID before deciding what to do next.
+
+This is a finite CLI process, not a daemon, new timer, Desktop window opener, or
+scheduled 07:00 ACK. Ctrl-C stops its command; a result whose cancellation cannot be
+proved remains ambiguous. A separate CLI process is not controlled by a Desktop Stop
+button. There is no execution-time deadline promise. Automation integration and its
+independent review/apply gates are separate from installing this command.
+
 `--delivery-mode` controls **how** the notifier reacts to a terminal event. Every subscription is in one of three modes (`notify` is the default and the original behavior):
